@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+# TLS
+
+Funnel can serve its HTTP API and its gRPC API over TLS, and workers and nodes
+can verify the server when they connect to the gRPC API. TLS is off by default.
+
+## Server
+
+Set a PEM certificate chain and its private key. Both listeners (`HTTPPort` and
+`RPCPort`) then serve TLS only, with TLS 1.2 as the minimum version.
+
+```yaml
+Server:
+  TLSCertFile: /etc/funnel/tls/tls.crt
+  TLSKeyFile: /etc/funnel/tls/tls.key
+```
+
+The files are read when the server starts. Restart the server after replacing
+them. Setting only one of the two is an error.
+
+## Workers and Nodes
+
+The gRPC client used by workers, nodes and RPC event writers connects over TLS
+when `RPCClient.TLS` is true or `RPCClient.TLSCAFile` is set.
+
+```yaml
+RPCClient:
+  ServerAddress: funnel.example.com:9090
+  TLSCAFile: /etc/funnel/tls/ca.crt
+  Credential:
+    User: funnel
+    Password: abc123
+```
+
+- `TLS: true` verifies the server certificate against the system roots.
+- `TLSCAFile` verifies it against the CA certificates in that PEM file instead.
+  It is a path on the machine or container where the worker or node runs, and
+  the file must exist there.
+- `TLSServerName` sets the name to verify when it differs from the host in
+  `ServerAddress`.
+
+Over TLS the client sends its Basic credential to remote addresses without
+`FUNNEL_ALLOW_INSECURE_CREDENTIALS`.
+
+When the server serves TLS, put these `RPCClient` settings in the configuration
+that workers and nodes read. A worker or node without them cannot connect to
+the gRPC API. It fails and does not fall back to plaintext.
+
+## HTTP Clients
+
+Use an `https://` server URL with the CLI and other TES clients. For a private
+CA on Linux, set `SSL_CERT_FILE` to the CA file for the Funnel CLI. This file
+replaces the default CA bundle file. The CLI still reads the system certificate
+directories, such as `/etc/ssl/certs`, unless `SSL_CERT_DIR` is set.
+
+Health probes and metrics scrapes of `/healthz` and `/metrics` must also use
+HTTPS when TLS is on, because the HTTP port no longer serves plaintext.
